WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:02.711
- With the bases loaded and one out.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.757
- Baseball and statistics go way back.

00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:10.260
Almanacs are filled with stories

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.304
from games over the last 100 plus years. 

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:16.307
But answering deep questions intuitively 

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:18.685
has been a giant challenge.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.562
Like, grand slam big.

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:23.606
- Going back. That is gone!

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.151
The MLB has teamed with Google Cloud

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:27.694
to push what's possible 

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.863
with data on and off the field.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:31.906
And I'm on a mission to find out 

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:33.533
just how they've done it. 

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:39.956
- Play Ball! 

00:00:39.956 --> 00:00:41.166
- Is first to a left hander

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:43.168
and this one to a wide open space.

00:00:43.168 --> 00:00:44.961
McCann with a great diving stop 

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.963
and he hits Peterson for the out!

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:49.090
His first play as a first baseman!

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.176
- Thanks to the data analytics tools in the cloud,

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:53.011
answers to burning questions 

00:00:53.011 --> 00:00:55.055
that fans have are within reach now.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.015
So in a single game, 

00:00:57.015 --> 00:00:58.558
how many data points 

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.351
are you actually tracking?

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.062
- In an individual game, we'll capture 

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.355
25 million unique data points.

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:07.525
There are 2,430 regular season

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:08.651
games every year, 

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:10.111
so that creates unique challenges 

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:11.821
for us as we think about how to deliver 

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:12.906
that to our customers.

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:16.201
- What kind of data is 

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:17.744
needed to decode the game? 

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.454
- One of the more powerful things 

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:20.663
that has been done with Major League 

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:22.791
Baseball is by marrying 

00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:25.251
the data to the video with MLB Film Room.

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:27.045
Now we're getting to the point 

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:28.713
where you're tracking the ball, 

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:31.091
the players multiple times every second.

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:32.759
You really need to have 

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:33.718
the right instrumentation 

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:35.303
and then have the right technologies 

00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:36.429
to help you process that data 

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:37.806
and answer the types of questions 

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.057
you can get with that data. 

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.851
- There it is! It's a triple play!

00:01:42.393 --> 00:01:43.853
- What are you using to process 

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:45.146
that data on the field?

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:47.190
-So we actually are using Anthos,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.399
which is a deployed 

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.110
Kubernetes inside the ballpark. 

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.611
So we send the data from the ballpark 

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.321
Anthos Kubernetes to our Kubernetes

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:54.614
cluster running and Google Cloud. 

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.324
From there, we're able to scale it out 

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:57.909
and stored in Cloud SQL

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:58.910
for our database and

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.787
read it back into our Stats API 

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.330
We're able to serve it to millions of fans 

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:03.748
around the world in real time.

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:12.799
- Data and baseball is 

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.467
clearly a beautiful marriage, 

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.052
but I would like to dig deeper 

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:18.471
and find out what's under the hood.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.181
As the ball is moving,

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:21.766
can you walk me through the process 

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:23.143
of collecting the data 

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.018
and processing it?

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:25.520
Now, as soon as the pitch is released, 

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.354
we have twelve Hawkeye 

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:27.856
cameras around the stadium 

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.691
tracking everything about that pitch 

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:31.109
at 30 frames per second.

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:32.902
So that includes about 60 more data points 

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:34.070
about every single pitch. 

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.029
So we have processors 

00:02:35.029 --> 00:02:37.073
collecting all this data from Hawkeye,

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:38.366
putting it into our data center 

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:39.242
within the ballpark, 

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:39.909
and we're able to make 

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:41.870
these determinations using the raw data. 

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.163
The end of the season, it amounts to

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.206
about over 25 terabytes of data.

00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:46.583
So we can't easily query 

00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:48.084
this data inside the ballpark,

00:02:48.084 --> 00:02:49.752
so we need a highly scalable technology 

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:50.753
such as Bigtable to be able 

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:51.796
to store this fast 

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:53.089
and read it back out fast 

00:02:53.089 --> 00:02:54.340
in order to get it to our consumer 

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.466
products in real time.

00:02:57.135 --> 00:02:57.677
Bigtable is 

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.137
awesome to use for things 

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:00.555
like post tracking data. 

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:01.764
So one new, exciting project 

00:03:01.764 --> 00:03:02.515
that we're working on 

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:03.683
is what we're calling FieldVision.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.726
This will be a constant 

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:06.144
stream of post tracking data 

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:06.853
throughout the game, 

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.771
visible for a fan on their desktop 

00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:09.856
or their mobile phone. 

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:10.857
So using our API, 

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.317
we're able to front Bigtable

00:03:12.317 --> 00:03:13.568
with a scalable technology 

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:15.236
that we can serve to millions of fans .

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:17.197
- How do you process it 

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:18.740
further to make it available 

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.451
for the broadcasters and the APIs?

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:22.535
- Any time you watch a game, 

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.870
you'll see this live strike zone 

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.413
over the center of home plate. 

00:03:25.413 --> 00:03:25.997
It's imperative 

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:26.789
that we're able to deliver 

00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:28.333
that information to the broadcast trucks 

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:29.751
at very low latency. 

00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:31.544
So every night we have a batch job 

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.588
that runs and we use data flow as a key

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:35.215
mechanism to make this work. 

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:35.965
We're able to move data 

00:03:35.965 --> 00:03:37.008
out of Bigtable, 

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:39.219
out of Cloud SQL, into GCS buckets,

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.428
and then from GCS buckets

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:41.471
into BigQuery. 

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:42.889
Once the data lands in BigQuery, 

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:44.474
we're able to give that to analysts 

00:03:44.474 --> 00:03:46.184
and share that with all 30 of the clubs.

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:54.567
- How could teams take 

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.361
advantage of all this data?

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.571
What does it mean to everyday operations?

00:03:58.696 --> 00:04:02.242
So how do MLB teams actually use data?

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.618
- Basically, you're pulling in data 

00:04:03.618 --> 00:04:05.662
from the API for everything

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:07.664
from reviewing major league on field 

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:10.166
performance, to player acquisitions,

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:12.001
to running our models. 

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:13.503
- What do you do with the data? 

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:15.171
- We're ingesting the data 

00:04:15.171 --> 00:04:16.089
from the various stats 

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.049
API resources that we have available.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:20.134
Regular gumbo feeds, the pose data,

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:21.678
and then we're aggregating it 

00:04:21.678 --> 00:04:23.263
and basically filtering it down 

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:24.722
into actionable information 

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:26.182
for our personnel. 

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:27.517
- What are some of the tools 

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:29.477
that you use to gain the insights? 

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:32.272
I'm not sure I want to answer that one. 

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:35.233
- So I tried to get John to tell me 

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:36.609
how his team is using 

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:37.902
specific Statcast 

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.404
data to get better players 

00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:40.989
and improve performance, 

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:43.074
but he wasn't willing to spill the tea.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:44.826
Competitive advantage and all.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.376
- Slider in, fastball in,

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:54.919
changeup, curveball, 

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:56.045
partridge in a pear tree. 

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:57.505
It makes me wonder:

00:04:57.505 --> 00:04:59.924
how can analysts use the cloud to help

00:04:59.924 --> 00:05:01.843
tell the whole story of baseball? 

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:03.219
And what about announcers? 

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:04.846
How do they consume this data 

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:06.889
and translate it to a fan 

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:08.349
who might not care much 

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:10.226
about that underlying data? 

00:05:10.226 --> 00:05:12.478
- I say this many times on MLB Now:

00:05:12.729 --> 00:05:14.230
this is not math class,

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.565
this is baseball, 

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:17.525
and it's always about asking 

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:18.693
the question that you want 

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:20.445
answered in the ballgame

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:22.113
and how you find answers now,

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:23.948
frequently, is in the data. 

00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:25.992
- And so when you're looking at this data,

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:27.702
what is the type of information 

00:05:27.702 --> 00:05:29.120
you're looking for? 

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.246
- It depends whether I'm 

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:31.748
looking for an extreme, 

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:33.124
a specific instance, 

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:34.709
like a longest career homerun 

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:35.877
or a fastest pitch, 

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:37.253
or whether I'm looking at something 

00:05:37.253 --> 00:05:38.880
that spans more of a career. 

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.298
That's where I would use 

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.550
any combination of a website,

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.885
like a baseball Savant,

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:45.803
FanGraphs, a baseball reference. 

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:47.597
Statcast allows us to measure 

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:48.848
everything on the baseball field. 

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.808
We can tell you how fast a pitch was, 

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:52.435
how hard a ball was hit. 

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:54.812
We can tell you how far a homerun went,

00:05:54.812 --> 00:05:56.564
and those are the things that I think 

00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:58.066
can really speak to fans. 

00:05:58.358 --> 00:05:59.734
- Now I get it. 

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:01.652
A massive amount of data from 

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:04.030
the field is captured, processed,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.656
and published as APIs 

00:06:05.656 --> 00:06:08.409
to power tools like Savant, Film Room,

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:10.286
and internal and external research.

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:13.206
Data and analytics in the 

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:15.208
cloud are powerful tools,

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:16.751
and it's been amazing learning 

00:06:16.751 --> 00:06:17.710
how it's applied 

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.754
in the sports world today. 

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:21.881
I am so excited for the future

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.092
of sports data analytics.

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:26.094
Thanks for joining me on this journey.

